Abstract = {Much of what politicians do, we maintain in this paper, is driven by
   their belief in the power of media, which motivates their desire to be
   featured in news coverage. Our argument rests upon recent advances in
   communication theory, stressing ``the influence on presumed media
   influence{''} (Gunther, Albert C., and J. Douglas Storey. 2003. ``The
   Influence of Presumed Influence.{''} Journal of Communication
   35(2):199-215) and contributes to our understanding of the mediatization
   of politics. Combined data from a survey of Israeli members of Knesset
   (MKs; n = 56), parliamentary reporters' (n = 20) rankings of MKs' media
   motivations, Knesset records of MKs' political activity, and data on the
   frequency of MKs' news appearances were used to test this argument.
   Structural equation modeling revealed that politicians' belief in the
   power of media increases their motivation and effort to appear in media
   coverage, which in turn is related both to greater media prominence and
   to more parliamentary activity. These results are discussed in light of
   their implications for both our understanding of political actors and
   the role of journalists.},
